    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i tried running a local docker run with the same image and the file is definitely there. so maybe it is a k8s thing. could it be a volume mount overwriting the /app directory or something with the liveness/readiness probes? how would i check if the probes are causing the restart loop?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A volume mount overwriting `/app` is a possibility, but it's less common than a simple path mismatch, though you should check your PersistentVolumeClaim definitions just in case. For the probes, if your liveness probe is failing immediately upon startup, it will cause the pod to restart, which looks exactly like a `CrashLoopBackOff`. You need to check the probe configuration in your Deployment manifest, specifically the `initialDelaySeconds` and the path the probe is hitting. If the application takes longer to initialize than the probe allows, it fails, leading to the restart loop.",
      "source": "target",
